export default "[Flo](/compare/alternatives/flo-app-alternative/) launched Anonymous Mode in September 2022, three months after the Supreme Court's Dobbs decision overturned Roe v. Wade. The feature arrived at a moment when users were actively asking whether a period tracker could be used as evidence in a state investigation. Flo's answer was Anonymous Mode.\n\nWe built Floriva because we kept finding the same gap when we read through how Anonymous Mode actually works. It does one specific thing. It does not do several other things that its name suggests. And the distinction between those two categories is exactly the distinction between policy-based privacy and architectural privacy.\n\n## What Anonymous Mode actually changes\n\nAnonymous Mode removes your name and email from the cycle data stored on Flo's servers. For users who opt in, period dates, symptoms, and pregnancy data are stored on Flo's infrastructure without the direct identifiers Flo would otherwise hold alongside them.\n\nThat is the full scope of the change. It is a server-side labeling change. Flo's engineering team stops attaching your name and email to the records your app uploads.\n\nFlo positions Anonymous Mode as a response to the post-Dobbs risk environment. The feature description emphasizes that cycle data is no longer linked to a user profile. Users looking for a signal that Flo took the post-Roe risk seriously often point to Anonymous Mode as evidence.\n\n## What Anonymous Mode does not change\n\nFlo is still a cloud-based app. Your cycle data still leaves your phone. It still travels to Flo's servers. It still sits in a database that Flo operates.\n\nAnonymous Mode does not cover data Flo already collected. If you used Flo for a year and then turned on Anonymous Mode, the year of prior data stays in Flo's systems under its original identifiers. The feature only applies forward.\n\nAnonymous Mode does not prevent a [subpoena](/resources/guides/can-police-access-period-tracker-data/) from reaching Flo's servers. A court order targeting Flo's records can compel the company to produce whatever it holds. If the subpoena specifies a device, an account, or an IP address range, the pseudonymized cycle data can often be linked back to a person through other logs Flo retains.\n\nAnonymous Mode does not prevent third-party SDKs from receiving data. Flo tightened SDK usage after the FTC action, but the feature itself does not block SDK data flows. Any privacy gains from reduced SDK sharing come from Flo's current policy and the FTC consent order, not from Anonymous Mode.\n\n## Why pseudonymization is not the same as privacy\n\nRemoving a name from a record is not the same as making the record anonymous. Pseudonymization is a technical step that strips direct identifiers. Re-identification is the known limitation of that step.\n\nAcademic research on pseudonymized datasets has repeatedly shown that a small number of auxiliary data points is often enough to match a pseudonymized record to a specific person. Four timestamped location points are enough to uniquely identify most people in a mobility dataset. The same structural problem applies to cycle data. A device ID, an IP address, an app install date, or a billing record can link pseudonymized cycle data back to the original user.\n\nFlo has not published a technical architecture document that third-party security researchers can audit to verify what re-identification is or is not possible inside its Anonymous Mode pipeline. Users are asked to trust the claim.\n\nJHU information security expert Anton Dahbura has noted that anonymous mode and other attempts to protect user health data may lure people into a false sense of security.\n\n## The $59.5M settlement happened with Anonymous Mode enabled\n\nFlo launched Anonymous Mode in September 2022. The class action against Flo Health, Google, Meta, and Flurry was actively litigated through 2025 while Anonymous Mode was live. The [$59.5M settlement](/resources/guides/flo-period-tracker-lawsuit-settlement/) that closed in September 2025 covered conduct from 2016 to 2019, but the point stands: Anonymous Mode did not prevent Flo from being a defendant. It did not prevent the company from paying out. It did not retroactively clean up the data Flo had already shared.\n\nA feature that decouples identifiers going forward cannot undo conduct that already happened. A feature that removes a name from a record cannot take a record off a server that law enforcement can reach.\n\nSource: Reuters reported the combined settlement on September 25, 2025. ClassAction.org confirmed the same figure.\n\n## What on-device storage solves that Anonymous Mode doesn't\n\nThe structural alternative to Anonymous Mode is [on-device storage](/resources/guides/on-device-storage-period-tracker/). On-device trackers store cycle data only on your phone. The data never reaches a company server. There is no database to pseudonymize, no record to subpoena, no employee who can be compelled to produce the data.\n\nThe difference matters because it removes the trust requirement. Anonymous Mode asks you to trust that Flo's internal pipeline actually decouples your identity from your cycle data, that pseudonymization holds against re-identification, and that future policy changes will not reattach identifiers. On-device storage requires none of that trust. The absence of a server-side copy is verifiable in a way that internal data handling is not.\n\nFloriva stores all cycle data on your device using encrypted local storage. No Floriva server holds your reproductive health data. There is no server-side mode to opt into and no anonymization claim to audit, because there is no readable central copy in the first place.";
